WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:05.780
- The Redwood Forest is
a very special place.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:08.860
It's the darkest forest on Earth.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.940
The trees are so tall, they
create their own shadows,

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.640
and in those shadows,
there are a lot of secrets.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.223
(gentle music)

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.290
My name is Dave Kuty. I'm a docent here

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.640
at Henry Cowell Redwoods State Park.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.110
I consider myself kind
of a volunteer naturalist

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.310
and redwood researcher.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.700
Before coming to Henry Cowell,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.070
I was a engineer in Silicon Valley.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.683
But, I just love the redwoods.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.140
I've always been an
outdoor kind of person.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.310
I've been coming here for 40 years.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.530
I come so often now I
swear I could almost tell

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:48.830
when a single branch fell.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.470
These are the coastal
redwood, Sequoia sempervirens.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.683
It is the tallest tree
on Earth by species.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:01.800
Their wood is very, very interesting.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.310
It's red in color.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.150
It's very complex, genetically,

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.863
and you can have some that
are not like all the others.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.330
Albino redwoods have been
called ghosts of the forest.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.833
We have found 13 albinos in this park.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.510
There are not very many,
not when you consider

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:21.773
there's two million
redwood trees out there.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.590
I really know albinos.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.573
I know where they all are, and
I continue to look for more.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.500
Albino redwoods have a
very, very soft nature.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.810
They aren't as structurally
sound as a normal redwood,

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:44.970
and we keep trying to
understand why they form here.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.240
When we first started
looking at these things

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.970
we said, "OK, they're
genetically different."

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:52.140
But it could be contamination.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.020
So, we have done some testing.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.950
Found that these guys
actually collect more nickel

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.530
than these guys.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.080
Nickel would normally kill chlorophyll,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.610
but the albino redwood
doesn't have any chlorophyll,

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.203
so it doesn't hurt it at all.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.340
So, where's the source of nickel?

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.993
Well, we're on a railroad.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.980
Some researchers think
it is acting like a liver

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.910
filtering out the toxic components

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.610
protecting the rest of the forest.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.790
When I first started here,
I started telling everybody

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.900
where they were, and then
I started finding pieces

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.940
of albino redwood on the ground.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.899
People would go and touch and fold and

00:02:31.899 --> 00:02:36.530
break off pieces, and I realized
that I couldn't do that.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.240
I could not be open with people

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.810
because they were gonna
go in and destroy it.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.643
So, their location has been kept secret.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.900
Redwoods are a treasure of the Earth.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.163
They really are.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.280
And the more you study
them, the more you realize

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.250
how unique they are and
how adaptable they are.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.300
Part of the fascination
of the redwood forest

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.890
is its diversity.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.059
I continue to be fascinated by what I see.

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:07.642
(gentle music)

